# Bound /var/log/cathedral-validator. Installed as
# /etc/logrotate.d/cathedral-validator by README's "Supported systemd install
# (relay)"; picked up by the host's own logrotate timer/cron, so nothing here
# needs a unit of its own.
#
# Both files this covers are append-only and neither had any cap: on the live
# SN39 box validator-events.jsonl reached 888 KB in nine days and would have
# kept going until the filesystem stopped it. The failure that costs an
# operator money is a validator that cannot write, and a full /var is one of
# the ways to get there.
#
# WHAT IS **NOT** ROTATED, and must never be added here: the durable submission
# state. /var/lib/cathedral-validator/journal-*.json and the lane state file
# carry the monotonic fences, the anti-rollback watermarks and the signed
# attempt journal. Truncating or renaming one of those is how a validator
# double-submits. This file names /var/log only.
#
# copytruncate is mandatory, not a preference. scaffold/events.py
# `_open_secure_append` opens each stream ONCE, with
# O_WRONLY|O_APPEND|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW, and `EventLogger` holds that descriptor
# for the life of the process. A rename-and-create rotation would leave the
# running validator writing into the renamed inode until its next restart,
# so the live path would sit at 0 bytes and `cathedral-mismatch-check` would
# (correctly) alert that it is blind. copytruncate keeps the inode, so the
# descriptor, its owner and its mode all survive, and because the descriptor is
# O_APPEND the next write lands at offset 0 of the truncated file rather than
# leaving a sparse hole.
#
# copytruncate's cost is a millisecond-wide window between the copy and the
# truncate in which one record can be lost. That is the right trade for
# telemetry: nothing in the submission path reads these files back, and the
# fences that do decide whether a write may happen are in /var/lib, above.
#
# delaycompress is load-bearing, not tidiness. For up to one tick interval
# (~25 min) after a rotation the live journal can hold no record at all, and
# both readers decide liveness from the newest record they can see:
# deploy/sn39/cathedral-mismatch-check, and scaffold/health.py `read_tail`
# behind `cathedral-validator status`. Both fall back to the single
# most recent rotated generation, and both read plain text. delaycompress is
# what keeps that generation uncompressed as `<name>.jsonl.1`; compressing it
# immediately would blind them for that window instead — a red liveness alert
# on a perfectly healthy validator.
#
# su is required because /var/log/cathedral-validator is 0750
# cathedral-validator:cathedral-validator-log (LogsDirectory= in
# cathedral-validator-sn39-relay.service, and the matching tmpfiles contract),
# not root-owned, and logrotate refuses to rotate inside a directory it does
# not own unless it is told which identity to drop to.
#
# Verify a change here without waiting for the timer:
#   logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.d/cathedral-validator
/var/log/cathedral-validator/*.jsonl {
    su cathedral-validator cathedral-validator-log
    daily
    maxsize 64M
    rotate 14
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
    compress
    delaycompress
    # No `create`: it would mint a new inode the running validator's held
    # descriptor does not point at. copytruncate reuses the original, so the
    # rotated copies inherit its owner and mode (0600 for the raw journal, 0640
    # for the sanitized status projection) and rotation never widens who can
    # read the raw stream.
}
